  "content": {
    "title": "MLS scores: Takeaways from Austin FC's 1-1 draw with Atlanta United",
    "text": "Austin FC midfielder Owen Wolff (33) and El Paso Locomotive FC midfielder Frank Daroma (8) battle for the ball in the second half as Austin takes on El Paso in the first round of the U.S. Open Cup at Q2 Stadium in Austin, Wednesday, May 7, 2025. Austin won the game 3-2 with a second-half comeback.Sara Diggins/American-StatesmanAustin FC broke its three-game MLS losing streak on Wednesday, but not in the fashion it wanted to. In a game at Q2 Stadium between two teams in desperate need of a win, neither got it, as the Verde & Black tied Atlanta United FC 1-1. Article continues below this adAustin FC looked poised to take three points after it took a 1-0 lead into second-half stoppage time thanks to Brandon Vazquez’s brilliant free-kick goal in the 55th minute, but the visitors used a great combination of passes that ended with Jamal Thiaré’s easy tap in of a cross for the equalizer. Here are some key takeaways from the match as Austin FC (5-6-2, 17 points) has another quick turnaround hosting Vancouver on Saturday: Opportunity lost for Austin FCThe common theme among Austin FC coach Nico Estévez and his players following the match was that they let one slip away. Article continues below this adThey weren't wrong.Though Atlanta’s goal was due to quality play, any tying goal given up late in the second half, let alone stoppage time, is a gut punch. “There’s a bitterness,” right back Jon Gallagher admitted. Article continues below this adIf there’s a criticism of Austin FC’s defense on the play, it’s that Atlanta midfielder Saba Lobjanidze was running wide open, which continued a theme from Saturday's 2-1 loss at Cincinnati. He took a phenomenal chip pass from Miguel Almirón before eventually providing the cross to Thiaré.Both Estévez and Gallagher said the Verde & Black were too spread out on the play, which started with a long pass from goalie Brad Guzan and chested from Thiaré to Almirón.“We just need to be more cohesive,” Gallagher said. “We needed to be more compact. … It ultimately could have been stopped.” The lack of a second goal hurtAustin FC should have been in a position to where a goal by Atlanta didn’t matter. Article continues below this adThe Verde & Black dictated play for most of the second half, constantly had possession on Atlanta’s side of the field and outshot the visitors 18-8 for the match. Yet a second goal never came. Austin FC let Atlanta hang around, which ultimately cost it two points. It’s something that clearly bothered Estévez. “We have to learn that we have to score goals when we have momentum,” said Estévez, who was more blunt in his postgame press conference than usual. “We have to have the killer mentality.” Is Brandon Vazquez getting hot?The positives from this game are that Austin FC avoided a noteworthy losing streak and Vazquez seems to be heating up. He scored for the third straight game and was a constant threat to the Atlanta defense. Article continues below this adIf Vazquez consistently plays like he did Wednesday, that will only mean good things for Austin FC, but he’s going to need some help on offense. Particularly from fellow forward Myrto Uzuni, who had a good work rate, but didn’t offer much in being a goal-scoring threat. However, it was a big missed opportunity for three points for Austin FC, especially with a match looming against the best team in MLS in Vancouver, who will enter Saturday on a full week of rest. Man of the match: Brandon Vazquez Vazquez’s goal showed why Austin FC brought him in over the winter. It was a free kick worthy of some of the better leagues in Europe. Article continues below this adAustin FC player gradesForward: Brandon Vazquez 8, Myrto Uzuni 5, Osman Bukari 5.5Midfield: Owen Wolff 6, Besard Sabovic 5.5, Ilie Sánchez 5Article continues below this adDefense: Guilherme Biro 6.5, Julio Cascante 6, Oleksandr Svatok 6, Jon Gallagher 8Subs: Zan Kolmanic 4.5, Jáder Obrian 5, Nicolás Dubersarsky NA, CJ Fodrey NA, Diego Rubio NAAustin FC midfielder Besard Sabovic and Atlanta United FC midfielder Bartosz Slisz battle for the ball during the second half of Wednesday night's 1-1 draw at Q2 Stadium.Scott Wachter/Imagn ImagesGet the hookup and your Horns Up with Longhorns coverage.Austin FC and high school sports reporterColby Gordon has written for the Austin American-Statesman since 2018. Covering Austin FC since late in its inaugural season and a member of the preps beat with Rick Cantu, his previous work has appeared in the Kansas City Star, Dallas Morning News, Toronto Star and Independence Examiner, just to name a few. Originally from the Midwest, he earned degrees in mass communications and education prior to beginning his newspaper career.",
